  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Mississippi has $5.4B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state has 75,609 awards with an average award size of $70.8K. Obligations fell 33.1% year over year. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer with $4.6B in obligations, while Ship Building and Repairing accounts for $3.6B of industry spending. FY2026 to date is a partial fiscal-year total and follows prior annual obligations of $4.9B in FY21, $5B in FY22, $8.7B in FY23, $6.5B in FY24, and $8B in FY25. Major vendors active in FY2026 to date include HUNTINGTON INGALLS INCORPORATED with $3.5B and V2X AEROSPACE LLC with $420M. Other significant agency buyers include the National Aeronautics and Space Administration with $235M and the Department of Veterans Affairs with $144M. The top industries beyond Ship Building and Repairing include Other Support Activities for Air Transportation with $472M and Facilities Support Services with $131M.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contracting in Mississippi changed year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 33.1% year over year. Mississippi recorded $5.4B in FY2026 to date after annual obligations of $8B in FY25 and $6.5B in FY24. The largest single-year total in the listed sequence was $8.7B in FY23."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What drives the bulk of federal spending in Mississippi?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense drives the bulk of spending with $4.6B in obligations in FY2026 to date. Ship Building and Repairing is the largest industry with $3.6B in obligations, supported by top vendors such as HUNTINGTON INGALLS INCORPORATED with $3.5B and V2X AEROSPACE LLC with $420M."
      }
    ]
  },
